1
00:00:06,988 --> 00:00:14,873
JAMIE: Merck's cancer vaccine just proved it works—stock's up twelve point six percent—and the Fed's about to make borrowing for literally everything else cost more.

2
00:00:14,873 --> 00:00:41,367
MAYA: Meta's down as the child safety trial kicks off—turns out 'Profits won' doesn't land as a courtroom defense. Today Arturo Béjar takes the stand with specifics on how Meta's algorithm amplifies harmful content to minors, and we're moving from opening arguments to actual testimony about whether the company knowingly tuned its recommendation engine to keep kids scrolling through worse stuff for engagement. The market's already betting that no amount of quarterly earnings can outrun a witness explaining, under oath, how the math works.

3
00:00:41,367 --> 00:01:09,123
ALEX: S&P up point two one percent, Nasdaq up point one six—we're in that weird zone where the market's basically shrugging. But look at the actual splits: Merck's up twelve point six on that cancer vaccine win, Accenture's up five point nine two, Salesforce up five point zero seven. Meanwhile Broadcom's down four point six one, AMD's down three point seven one—that's the real story. Semiconductors are getting hammered because investors are starting to wonder if the AI capex cycle can actually justify the valuations they've been paying.

4
00:01:09,123 --> 00:01:15,431
JAMIE: So healthcare's the one thing that works right now because it's not pretending to be smarter than it is?

5
00:01:15,431 --> 00:01:27,416
ALEX: Pretty much. Merck proved something works. Broadcom's asking investors to trust that the next generation of chips will be worth two hundred billion in new infrastructure spend. One's a clinical trial. The other's a spreadsheet with confidence.

6
00:01:27,416 --> 00:01:41,925
MAYA: And the VIX just collapsed six percent, which is wild because normally when semiconductors tank that hard you'd see traders freaking out, but no—it's like everyone agreed at the same time that this was boring bad news, not scary bad news. The money's just rotating.

7
00:01:41,925 --> 00:01:49,179
JAMIE: Calmest panic—that's what happens when you've already priced in that the thing you were excited about three months ago might not work.

8
00:01:49,179 --> 00:02:02,111
ALEX: Moderna's personalized cancer vaccine prevents cancer recurrence in phase two data. That's not a statistical whisper—that's a clinical result that moves the needle on actual patient survival, not just tumor markers or whatever metric was convenient for the press release.

9
00:02:02,111 --> 00:02:08,104
JAMIE: That's the kind of number that makes oncologists sit up. How much is Moderna stock moving on this?

10
00:02:08,104 --> 00:02:29,236
MAYA: It's soaring, but here's the thing—this is a personalized vaccine. So Moderna has to sequence your tumor, grow your own custom shot, and you're looking at months of manufacturing before you're even getting the injection. It's not like a vial off the shelf. The efficacy is real, but the logistics are basically asking patients to wait while a lab plays 3D printer with their cancer genome.

11
00:02:29,236 --> 00:02:48,791
ALEX: That's why the trial was paired with Merck's Keytruda—they're not selling Moderna's vaccine as a standalone cure. They're selling it as a booster to an existing immunotherapy. The company doesn't have to solve the manufacturing speed problem on day one if the regulatory pathway is 'add this to what already works.' That's how you get approval fast and market penetration even faster.

12
00:02:48,791 --> 00:02:53,522
JAMIE: So Moderna's not reinventing the wheel, they're just putting a better tire on it.

13
00:02:53,522 --> 00:03:08,346
MAYA: And the tire costs somewhere in the range of what a small car costs, because bespoke cancer medicine isn't cheap. The real question is whether insurance companies start covering it as a standard-of-care add-on or whether this stays a wealth filter for the next three years.

14
00:03:08,346 --> 00:03:25,063
ALEX: Moderna's betting on the former. If this clears FDA review—and the data's strong enough that it probably will—then the precedent shifts. You're not just approving a drug, you're approving a category: personalized immunotherapy as a standard next step in cancer treatment. That's worth billions to everyone in the space, not just Moderna.

15
00:03:25,063 --> 00:03:35,787
ALEX: Treasury Secretary Bessent just moved to curb Treasury yields. It's a direct counter to Trump's rate complaints, which means Bessent's basically saying the Fed doesn't get to own the bond market conversation anymore.

16
00:03:35,787 --> 00:03:42,410
JAMIE: So the Treasury Secretary is fighting the Fed chair through the bond market instead of just picking up the phone.

17
00:03:42,410 --> 00:03:54,080
ALEX: Right. The market's already pricing in that Bessent wins this one because his move actually cooled the selloff. The Fed can talk all it wants about independence, but Treasury moves affect yields faster than jawboning does.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/19/bessent-treasury-buybacks-yields-warsh-fed.html

18
00:03:54,080 --> 00:04:04,173
MAYA: T-Mobile literally severed the cable to Chinese hackers. That's the headline—'chopped a cable'—like they walked into the server room with garden shears and solved cybersecurity the way you'd stop a robocall.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/19/t-mobile-chopped-a-cable-to-expel-chinese-hackers-from-its-network/

19
00:04:04,173 --> 00:04:10,166
JAMIE: Did they actually cut a physical cable or is that just how the press release is framing it?

20
00:04:10,166 --> 00:04:28,775
MAYA: They cut a physical cable. The Chinese-backed hackers were using a specific network connection to stay inside T-Mobile's systems, and once the company identified it early enough, they just yanked it. No ransomware, no data exfil, no months of forensics. Catch the intruder, pull the plug, move on. It's weirdly the best-case scenario for a breach that almost happened.

21
00:04:28,775 --> 00:04:37,921
ALEX: Which means the real lesson here is that early detection beats expensive remediation every time, but most companies won't invest in detection until after they've already been breached.

22
00:04:37,921 --> 00:04:42,653
MAYA: OpenAI's CFO just told employees the company will be public in 2027, or sooner.'
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/19/open-ai-ipo-timing-2027-friar.html

23
00:04:42,653 --> 00:04:45,807
JAMIE: Is there an IPO timeline that's actually closer than that?

24
00:04:45,807 --> 00:04:51,799
MAYA: No one knows. But by saying '2027 or sooner,' Friar gets to look decisive while committing to nothing.

25
00:04:51,799 --> 00:04:59,054
ALEX: Target's fiscal second-quarter sales beat expectations. The company raised full-year guidance. And the actual driver is a tariff refund from the government.

26
00:04:59,054 --> 00:05:03,154
JAMIE: So Target's turnaround is being held up by a one-time accounting adjustment.

27
00:05:03,154 --> 00:05:27,756
MAYA: Arturo Béjar's on the stand right now—he was a Meta engineer who spent years watching the algorithm get tuned to maximize engagement, and he's testifying that the company knew exactly what it was doing. He's bringing internal emails, meeting notes, the whole thing. And Meta's strategy so far is basically 'we made money, so what's the problem,' which is not the energy you want in a federal courtroom where the judge doesn't care about your quarterly earnings.

28
00:05:27,756 --> 00:05:48,257
ALEX: Meta's defense is structured around the idea that they were optimizing for a business metric, not deliberately harming kids. But Béjar's testimony is specifically about the moment when the company had data showing the algorithm was amplifying harmful content, and instead of deprioritizing it, they kept it in the feed because the engagement was too good. That's not a gray area—that's a documented choice.

29
00:05:48,257 --> 00:06:05,604
JAMIE: So the stock's down, the CFO's got nothing, and now a guy who actually built the thing is in court explaining how the math works. Meta spent years arguing that they care about safety—they've got a whole Responsible AI team—and Béjar's job is to show that the safety team lost every argument that mattered.

30
00:06:05,604 --> 00:06:17,905
JAMIE: On the tape tomorrow: we've got earnings from Analog Devices, Bill Holdings, and a few others hitting on the nineteenth—but the real watch is whether semiconductor companies can actually explain their valuations without the word 'AI' appearing once.

31
00:06:17,905 --> 00:06:33,360
ALEX: Analog Devices on the nineteenth is the litmus test. They're pure analog infrastructure—power management, signal processing, the stuff that makes everything else work. If they guide down because data center capex is slowing, that tells you the AI spending boom is narrowing faster than anyone wants to admit.

32
00:06:33,360 --> 00:06:46,607
MAYA: And Bill Holdings is also reporting the nineteenth—they're enterprise software, so if their guidance is weak, you get two different parts of the stack saying the same thing at the same time. That's when the market stops rotating and starts selling.

33
00:06:46,607 --> 00:06:53,230
JAMIE: So we're either getting 'everything's fine, here's the proof' or 'we have no idea what's coming next.' There's no middle.

34
00:06:53,230 --> 00:07:00,800
ALEX: Merck's cancer vaccine worked, so the stock got paid. Everything else is waiting to find out if the thing they bought actually does.

35
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:03,954
MAYA: Meanwhile Broadcom's trading like it forgot how to multiply.

36
00:07:03,954 --> 00:07:08,370
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
